# Terminates TLS for both public-facing services — §9.2's own diagram: a
# single Caddy edge, everything else reachable only on the compose
# network. Two domains, not one domain with path-based routing: the gateway's
# origin is what every integrator pins and every sealed request is addressed
# to, and the operator console keeps its paired browser key in IndexedDB,
# which the browser scopes to the console's own origin. Merging them would
# move both for no gain. (Until VS-4 there was a second reason: the gateway
# was an OpenID issuer whose discovery document is only well-defined at an
# origin's root. The issuer is deleted; the two origins stay.)
#
# Both domain vars are required (deploy/docker-compose.yml's own
# `${SMS_GATEWAY_DOMAIN:?}` / `${SMS_ADMIN_DOMAIN:?}` fail the whole stack
# rather than start half-configured) — set them in deploy/.env.
#
# Real deploy, real DNS pointed at this VM: nothing further needed below.
# Caddy's automatic HTTPS (ACME via Let's Encrypt, using CADDY_ACME_EMAIL)
# takes over on its own the moment it can complete an HTTP-01 or TLS-ALPN
# challenge against a real hostname.
#
# Local/demo run with no public DNS: swap the commented `tls internal`
# line in for the real cert flow in both blocks below — Caddy then mints
# its own local CA and self-signed certs. See
# docs/runbooks/deployment.adoc for trusting that CA on your own machine.
#
# #156 — rate limiting `/dlr/{providerKey}` at this edge.
# `docs/architecture.md` §4.6 assigns this duty to "the reverse proxy".
# `rate_limit` is a third-party Caddy module
# (github.com/mholt/caddy-ratelimit) — stock `caddy:2-alpine` does not
# have it (confirmed: `caddy version`/`caddy list-modules` in that image
# has no `http.handlers.rate_limit`). This deploy tree builds its own
# image from `deploy/caddy.Dockerfile` instead — see that file's own
# header for exactly what changes and why.
#
# Every numeric limit below is env-tunable with a documented default (see
# `deploy/.env.example`'s own "Caddy rate limiting" section for the
# reasoning behind each number) so an operator can retune without a
# rebuild — only the *keys* (what a limiter buckets requests by) are
# hardcoded, since getting those wrong is a security property, not a
# capacity tuning knob.
#
# **`/login` and `/token` are served again (VSA-4a) when `human_login` or
# `machine_tokens` is configured, and have one zone here, `auth_per_source`.**
# Until VS-4 the gateway embedded an OpenID Provider whose `/token` was limited at
# this edge (per source IP, plus an aggregate bucket) and again in the application
# (per `client_id`); VS-4 deleted it, and this file said so. VSA-3a/VSA-4a put the
# routes back behind the `op:` block, and a deployment that serves neither method
# still answers them with the gateway's own 404, which costs it nothing worth
# bounding. The zone is per source only, on purpose: the dimensions the edge
# cannot see (the `client_id` and the code in a form body, the email in a JSON
# body) are the application's, `token_rate_limit.rs` and `login.rs`, and a
# per-email or per-code bucket here would mean reading a request body in Caddy.
# What this zone is for is the one thing the application cannot do: it sees one
# peer (this proxy) for everybody, so it has no per-source limit of its own.
# Every other route is a sealed request: an unsigned one is refused at the
# envelope layer before any database work, and the application's two per-source
# and per-principal limiters (`sms_api::router`) bound the rest.
#
# `/dlr/{providerKey}` is worth rate limiting because it is unauthenticated
# at this layer by design (a provider cannot sign a request, and each
# provider authenticates its callback its own way — #34's own module doc),
# and it is a Postgres write on every call (`dlr.rs` matches against
# `Message` and writes a `DeliveryReceipt` per request). `providerKey` is a
# URL *path* segment (`backends/apps/sms-gateway/src/dlr.rs` mounts
# `POST /dlr/{providerKey}`), so `{http.request.uri.path}` is one of Caddy's
# most ordinary placeholders — no body parsing, no third-party risk.
#
# No zone below trusts `X-Forwarded-For`/`client_ip` — deliberately:
# `deploy/docker-compose.yml` publishes Caddy's own ports directly to the
# host (`grep -n 'ports:' -A3 deploy/docker-compose.yml`), there is no
# upstream load balancer or CDN in front of it in this topology, so
# `{http.request.remote.host}` (the actual TCP peer address Caddy
# accepted the connection from) is the real client IP and cannot be
# spoofed by a request header. If this deployment ever grows a real
# upstream proxy (a cloud LB, a CDN), these zones need `trusted_proxies`
# configured globally and their keys switched to a placeholder that
# honors it — keying on `remote.host` unchanged in that topology would
# silently bucket every request behind the LB into one IP, collapsing
# per-client isolation rather than merely losing it loudly.
#
# `ipv6_prefix 64` on the per-source zone: caddy-ratelimit's own README
# names the exact attack this defends — an attacker with a routed IPv6
# `/64` can cycle through billions of individual addresses to dodge a
# naive per-address bucket; masking to `/64` buckets the whole prefix
# together instead. No IPv4 equivalent is set — an attacker would need to
# actually control a `/N` block of IPv4 space to get the same effect,
# meaningfully rarer.
#
# Single Caddy instance in this compose stack (`deploy/docker-compose.yml`
# runs exactly one `caddy` service, no replicas) — caddy-ratelimit's
# in-memory state is authoritative as-is. A `distributed { }` block only
# matters the moment a second Caddy replica exists; add it then, not
# preemptively against a topology this stack doesn't have.

{
	email {$CADDY_ACME_EMAIL}
}

{$SMS_GATEWAY_DOMAIN} {
	# tls internal

	# Production-readiness audit N3: baseline security headers on every
	# response this edge sends, not just the API's own JSON error bodies.
	# `Strict-Transport-Security` matters most on the two origins Caddy
	# terminates TLS for (this file's own header explains why they're
	# two separate domains, not path-routed) — a browser that has ever
	# loaded either over HTTPS refuses a downgraded HTTP connection to
	# the same host for a year afterward, closing the classic
	# strip-to-HTTP MITM window. `X-Content-Type-Options: nosniff` stops
	# a browser from MIME-sniffing a response into executing as
	# something other than what `Content-Type` says. `Referrer-Policy` caps
	# what a cross-origin link leaks about this deployment's own URLs
	# (message ids, `client_id`s in a query string, if any ever end up
	# in one) to `strict-origin-when-cross-origin` — a conservative,
	# commonly-recommended default (OWASP Secure Headers Project), not
	# copied from anywhere else in this repo.
	header {
		Strict-Transport-Security "max-age=31536000; includeSubDomains"
		X-Content-Type-Options "nosniff"
		Referrer-Policy "strict-origin-when-cross-origin"
	}

	rate_limit {
		# Per-(IP, providerKey) budget on DLR callbacks. Sized against
		# §4.6's own documented constraint, not a round number: the
		# worker's `dispatch` role respects "Orange's hard 5 TPS cap" on
		# *outbound* submissions, so real inbound DLR traffic for one
		# provider is bounded by a small multiple of 5/s (a message can
		# receive more than one status callback — e.g. an intermediate
		# then a final state). 10 events/second leaves 2x headroom above
		# that 5 TPS ceiling before throttling engages.
		#
		# Keyed on the bare source IP, NOT the composite
		# `{remote.host}_{uri.path}` an earlier revision used. That
		# composite silently disabled `ipv6_prefix`: the module's
		# `applyNetworkPrefix` does `ip := net.ParseIP(key); if ip == nil
		# { return key }`, and `2001:db8::1_/dlr/orange_cm` never parses
		# as an IP, so masking was skipped and an IPv6 attacker could
		# rotate addresses inside a /64 to mint a fresh bucket per
		# request. Verified against the module source, not its docs.
		#
		# The cost is losing per-providerKey isolation, which buys
		# nothing today: `orange_cm` is the only provider wired up
		# (AGENTS.md), so the path dimension has exactly one value. When
		# a second provider lands (#63), add a second zone rather than
		# reinstating a composite key here — the IPv6 masking is the
		# security property, the path split is only fairness.
		zone dlr_per_source {
			match {
				path /dlr/*
			}
			key {http.request.remote.host}
			events {$RATE_LIMIT_DLR_PER_KEY_EVENTS:100}
			window {$RATE_LIMIT_DLR_PER_KEY_WINDOW:10s}
			ipv6_prefix 64
		}

		# Aggregate DLR budget, same defense-in-depth reasoning as
		# the per-source zone — bounds total Postgres write load from this route
		# regardless of how many distinct (IP, providerKey) buckets an
		# attacker spreads across. 4x the per-source ceiling: generous
		# enough that legitimate concurrent traffic across every
		# configured provider never trips it, since only one provider
		# (`orange_cm`) is wired up in this milestone (AGENTS.md).
		zone dlr_global {
			match {
				path /dlr/*
			}
			key dlr-aggregate
			events {$RATE_LIMIT_DLR_GLOBAL_EVENTS:200}
			window {$RATE_LIMIT_DLR_GLOBAL_WINDOW:10s}
		}

		# `/login` (an Argon2id verification per request) and `/token` (a client
		# lookup, and a signature check for a machine client): the paths a caller
		# can reach unauthenticated that cost real work. One bucket per source, shared
		# by the two, deliberately small: a person types a password a handful of
		# times and a machine client caches its token for fifteen minutes. Sized
		# below the application's aggregate `/login` bucket on purpose (see
		# `human_login.login_rate_limit.aggregate`): one source cannot spend that
		# budget alone, so a single bad actor cannot lock every operator out; it takes
		# at least `aggregate refill / (events / window)` distinct /64 prefixes.
		# `ipv6_prefix 64` for the same reason as `dlr_per_source`.
		zone auth_per_source {
			match {
				path /login /token
			}
			key {http.request.remote.host}
			events {$RATE_LIMIT_AUTH_PER_KEY_EVENTS:10}
			window {$RATE_LIMIT_AUTH_PER_KEY_WINDOW:60s}
			ipv6_prefix 64
		}

		log_key
	}

	# #70/#71: `/metrics` deliberately does not need a zone here. It never
	# reaches this `reverse_proxy` at all — sms-gateway's own `/metrics`
	# listener is a second, separate port (9090, default
	# SMS_METRICS_LISTEN_ADDR) this block never names, not a route on
	# 8080 — so there is nothing below for an attacker to even request
	# through this domain. See backends/crates/sms-api/src/metrics.rs's own module
	# doc for the full reasoning (the same "must not become a DoS
	# amplifier"/reconnaissance-value question `/readyz`'s R1 exception
	# already answers for a different route).
	reverse_proxy sms-gateway:8080
}

{$SMS_ADMIN_DOMAIN} {
	# tls internal

	# Production-readiness audit N3 — see {$SMS_GATEWAY_DOMAIN}'s own
	# identical block above for the full reasoning.
	header {
		Strict-Transport-Security "max-age=31536000; includeSubDomains"
		X-Content-Type-Options "nosniff"
		Referrer-Policy "strict-origin-when-cross-origin"
	}

	reverse_proxy admin:8080
}
